# Patterns.dev Extract — Design/Rendering/Performance Pattern Catalog

Purpose:
  Machine-usable extract of the patterns.dev free book catalog (design,
  rendering, performance patterns for vanilla JS + React, incl. AI UI
  Patterns) for SDDRA design-skill reference and AI-agent frontend work.

Owns: design/imported/patterns-dev-extract.sdd
ID: DESIGN-PATTERNSDEV

Domain: design
Level: L2
Status: EXTRACTED (CANDIDATE — see imported/INDEX.sdd [IMP3])

Extracted: 2026-09-07
Source: https://www.patterns.dev
Origin site authors: Lydia Hallie, Addy Osmani (https://www.patterns.dev/about)
License: CC BY-NC 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/) —
  see Rule [PD2]. Site stance: "design patterns are descriptive, not
  prescriptive" — a catalog for awareness, not a checklist (https://www.patterns.dev)

# ============================================================
# Section 1: Catalog Structure (verified from site navigation)
# Source: https://www.patterns.dev (homepage) + section sidebars at
#   https://www.patterns.dev/vanilla and https://www.patterns.dev/react
# ============================================================

Tracks:
  vanilla_design: Singleton, Proxy, Prototype, Observer, Module, Mixin,
    Mediator/Middleware, Flyweight, Factory, Command, Provider, Static Import
  vanilla_rendering: Islands Architecture, Animating View Transitions,
    Rendering Introduction
  vanilla_performance: Loading Sequence, Dynamic Import, Import On
    Visibility, Import On Interaction, Route-Based Splitting (homepage),
    Bundle Splitting, PRPL, Tree Shaking, Preload, Prefetch, Third-Party
    Loading, List Virtualization, Compressing JavaScript
  react_design: HOC, Hooks, Compound, Container/Presentational,
    Render Props, AI UI Patterns, React Stack Patterns (react-2026)
  react_rendering: Client-Side Rendering, Server-Side Rendering, Static
    Rendering, Incremental Static Generation, Progressive Hydration,
    Streaming SSR, React Server Components, Selective Hydration
  react_overviews: Overview of React.js, Overview of Next.js,
    Next.js Core Web Vitals (react/nextjs-vitals), React Stack 2025/2026
  vue: Introduction, Components, Async Components, Composables,
    Container/Presentational, Data Provider, Dynamic Components,
    Provide/Inject, Render Functions, Renderless Components,
    <script setup>, State Management
Notes:
  - "Selective Hydration" appears in React sidebar (/react/react-selective-hydration)
    but not on homepage cards; homepage additionally lists /react/nextjs-vitals.
  - Vue section exists on homepage; not requested for detail — names only.
  - Fetch failures: /ai and /ai-ui-patterns (root) returned 404; the AI UI
    Patterns chapter lives at /react/ai-ui-patterns. No pattern names invented.

# ============================================================
# Section 2: Key Patterns (name | intent | when | trade-off)
# Each line's URL is the pattern's chapter page under https://www.patterns.dev
# ============================================================

- Singleton | One shared global instance | Single config/cache/cache-point | Hidden global state, test coupling | /vanilla/singleton-pattern
- Proxy | Intercept/control interactions with target objects | Validation, lazy init, access control | Extra indirection layer | /vanilla/proxy-pattern
- Observer | Observables notify subscribers on events | Event-driven decoupling | Memory leaks if unsubscribe missed | /vanilla/observer-pattern
- Module | Split code into smaller reusable pieces | Always (ES modules baseline) | Namespace/encapsulation discipline | /vanilla/module-pattern
- Mediator/Middleware | Central object handles component communication | Complex many-to-many interactions | God-object risk | /vanilla/mediator-pattern
- Flyweight | Reuse existing instances for identical objects | Massive counts of similar objects | Cache/lookup overhead | /vanilla/flyweight-pattern
- Factory | Factory function creates objects | Construction logic varies by input | Extra abstraction layer | /vanilla/factory-pattern
- Islands Architecture | Ship static HTML, hydrate interactive "islands" | Content-heavy pages w/ sparse interactivity | Two rendering worlds to maintain | /vanilla/islands-architecture
- Dynamic Import | Import code on demand | Below-the-fold/heavy features | Waterfall delay on first use | /vanilla/dynamic-import
- Import On Visibility | Load non-critical parts when scrolled into viewport | Long lists, widgets | Needs IntersectionObserver fallbacks | /vanilla/import-on-visibility
- Import On Interaction | Load when user interacts with triggering UI | Below-the-fold heavy modals/editors | First-use latency hit | /vanilla/import-on-interaction
- Bundle Splitting | Split code into small reusable chunks | Large apps, better caching | More requests/roundtrips | /vanilla/bundle-splitting
- PRPL (Push/Render/Pre-cache/Lazy-load) | Optimize initial load end-to-end | Mobile-first perf audits | 2016 framing: server push dead, now preload/Early Hints + LCP/INP (see /vanilla/prpl) | /vanilla/prpl
- Tree Shaking | Eliminate dead code from bundle | Library consumers, ES-module code | Requires side-effect-free modules | /vanilla/tree-shaking
- Preload | Tell browser critical resources early | Fonts, above-fold JS/CSS | Over-preload wastes bandwidth | /vanilla/preload
- Prefetch | Fetch/cache resources likely needed soon | Next-route warmup | Guess wrong = wasted bytes | /vanilla/prefetch
- List Virtualization | Render only visible list items | Very long chat histories/feeds | Fixed/estimated row heights needed | /vanilla/virtual-lists
- HOC | Wrap component to inject cross-cutting behavior (auth, analytics) | Legacy/3rd-party composition | Wrapper hell, prop collisions | /react/hoc-pattern
- Hooks | Compose stateful/side-effect logic into reusable functions | Modern React default | Rules-of-hooks constraints | /react/hooks-pattern
- Compound Components | Cooperating components share implicit state | <select>-like flexible APIs | Implicit context magic | /react/compound-pattern
- Container/Presentational | Separate view from application logic | Testable pure UI layer | Boilerplate; hooks blur the line | /react/presentational-container-pattern
- Render Props | Supply rendering logic from outside via function-prop | Shared behavior, custom render | Nested-callback noise | /react/render-props-pattern
- Streaming SSR | Stream HTML as server renders | Fast first paint, SEO | Server complexity | /react/streaming-ssr
- React Server Components | Server-render intermediate output, no JS bundle cost | Data-heavy UIs; >20% bundle cuts reported | Client/server component split discipline | /react/react-server-components
- Selective Hydration | Hydrate priority parts first, stream others | TTI improvements on slow devices | Reorder/hydration pitfalls | /react/react-selective-hydration

# ============================================================
# Section 3: AI UI Patterns chapter (detail) — SDDRA priority
# Source: https://www.patterns.dev/react/ai-ui-patterns
# Implementations shown: Vite+React (Node/Express backend) and Next.js
# (App Router route handlers), using Vercel AI SDK + TailwindCSS.
# ============================================================

ArchEndpoint:
  - AI calls MUST go through a backend route (/api/chat): Next.js route
    handler or separate Express server behind Vite proxy — never client-side
    API keys (https://www.patterns.dev/react/ai-ui-patterns)
  - Streaming: Next.js native (StreamingTextResponse, Edge runtime) vs
    manual res.write() in Node — trade-off table in chapter
PromptManagement:
  - Conversation = message array [{role: user|assistant|system, content}]
    sent each request; system message sets behavior/persona; prepend on
    server or via initialMessages (https://www.patterns.dev/react/ai-ui-patterns)
  - useChat hook manages messages/input/handleSubmit; useCompletion for
    single-turn (no history) prompts
StreamingResponses:
  - stream:true → chunked delivery; client reads response.body reader,
    decodes, appends to last message — "AI typing" effect; auto-scroll
    container on new chunks; show TypingIndicator during stream
  - UX rationale: long answers must show partial results immediately
InputDebouncing:
  - Debounce (setTimeout/clearTimeout, ~500ms) before auto-query calls
    (autocomplete/AI validation); disable input + Send while isLoading to
    prevent overlapping requests scrambling message order
ErrorFallbacks:
  - try/catch around API calls; client onError callback; render inline
    error as part of conversation ("Sorry, something went wrong"); retry
    button resends last prompt; validate client-side (empty/too-long)
    before calling API; AI Elements has ErrorBoundary/ErrorMessage
StopButton:
  - useChat exposes stop() to cancel an in-flight stream when user sends
    a new query — cancellation is a named pattern in the chapter
AgenticTraceUI:
  - Chapter flags: when model emits reasoning traces + tool calls
    (agentic), chat UI "becomes more complex" — must render intermediate
    steps, function calls, structured outputs, not just text bubbles
    (cites https://www.lukew.com/ff/entry.asp?2135 via chapter image)
  - SDDRA mapping: agent frontends need trace/tool-call timeline views
    beyond plain chat bubbles (extract's inference from cited section)
ReusableComponents:
  - Presentational ChatMessage (role-styled bubbles), InputBox — decoupled
    from fetching logic; compose in ChatInterface
  - Vercel AI Elements prebuilt: Conversation (auto-scroll), Prompt,
    TypingIndicator, ErrorBoundary (https://vercel.com/academy/ai-sdk/ai-elements,
    cited by chapter)
StackContext:
  - React Stack Patterns chapter (https://www.patterns.dev/react/react-2026)
    covers 2025/2026 stack: Vite default build tool, Next.js default
    framework, TanStack Router (type-safe) vs React Router, TanStack
    Query for server state, Zustand/Redux Toolkit for global state,
    "vibe coding" AI-assisted workflow guidance

# ============================================================
# Section 4: Rules
# ============================================================

Rules:
  [PD1] Attribution REQUIRED: cite patterns.dev chapter URL whenever a
        pattern from this catalog informs SDDRA design/workflow output.
  [PD2] Patterns (concepts) are not ownable and may be applied freely;
        site text/code examples are CC BY-NC 4.0 — LINK, never copy
        verbatim into SDDRA docs or generated code (non-commercial clause).
  [PD3] Patterns are descriptive, not prescriptive (site's own stance):
        match pattern to SDDRA problem; a spec without the problem does
        not need the pattern. No checklist-driven application.
  [PD4] Every pattern claim in SDDRA specs MUST carry the source URL
        from Section 2/3; if a URL dies, mark stale, do not paraphrase
        from memory as fact.
  [PD5] AI-UI patterns (Section 3) map directly to SDDRA AI-agent
        frontend needs (chat, streaming, traces, stop, fallbacks);
        prefer them over invented interaction models when building
        agent-facing UI — deviations need rationale (ux-patterns.sdd rule).

